<section xml:id="section-activity-slo3">
  <title>Activity for SLO-3</title>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-slo3-review">
    <title>Conceptual Review</title>
    <p>
      You can test your learning from the video (and your own
      background knowledge) with the following review questions.
      Conceptual questions on quizes may be taken from these review
      questions. 
    </p>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why is L'Hôpital's rule useful for determining asymptotic
          order?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          In mathematics, we require proof of statements. We can
          roughly observe the asymptotic behaviour of functions and
          make a reasonable guess at the asymptotic orders. But to
          know something mathematically, we need to prove it.
          L'Hôpital's rule is a pretty efficient way of proving that
          one function grows faster than another, at least for the
          functions we are considering in this course. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-activity-hopitals-asymptotic">
    <title>Calculation Questions: Proving Asymptotic Orders</title>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use L'Hôpital's rule to prove that <m>\ln(\ln x)</m> grows
          slower than <m>\ln x</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          I setup the limit of the ratio of the two functions.
          <md>
            \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{\ln(\ln x)}{\ln x}
          </md>
          This is a limit of type <m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m>, so I
          can use L'Hôpital's rule. 
          <md>
            \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{\frac{d}{dx} \ln(\ln
            x)}{\frac{d}{dx} \ln x}
          </md>
          I do both derivatives. The numerator derivative is a chain
          rule derivatie. 
          <md>
            \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{\frac{1}{\ln x}
            \frac{1}{x}}{\frac{1}{x}}
          </md>
          Then I simplify the nested fraction. The <m>\frac{1}{x}</m>
          terms cancel each other out. 
          <md>
            \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{\ln x} = 0 
          </md>
          This is a limit where the numerator is fixed and the
          denominator grows without bound, so the limit is 0. I
          conclude that, in the original ratio, the numerator grows
          more slowly. Therefore, <m>\ln (\ln x)</m> grows more slowly
          than <m>\ln x</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
</section>
